  "generated_text": "To solve the expression \\(\\frac{1}{-6} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 + 8 \\cdot 2 \\div 8 \\cdot (7-1)\\), we need to follow the order of operations, often remembered by the acronym PEMDAS (Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication and Division (from left to right), Addition and Subtraction (from left to right)).\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Evaluate the expression inside the parentheses first:\n   \\[\n   7 - 1 = 6\n   \\]\n   So the expression now becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{-6} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 + 8 \\cdot 2 \\div 8 \\cdot 6\n   \\]\n\n2. Perform the multiplications and divisions from left to right:\n   - First, calculate \\(\\frac{1}{-6} \\cdot 6\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{1}{-6} \\cdot 6 = \\frac{1 \\cdot 6}{-6} = \\frac{6}{-6} = -1\n     \\]\n   - Next, calculate \\(8 \\cdot 2 \\div 8\\):\n     \\[\n     8 \\cdot 2 = 16\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     16 \\div 8 = 2\n     \\]\n   - Now, multiply the result by 6:\n     \\[\n     2 \\cdot 6 = 12\n     \\]\n   So the expression now simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   -1 \\cdot 7 + 12\n   \\]\n\n3. Perform the remaining multiplication and addition:\n   - Calculate \\(-1 \\cdot 7\\):\n     \\[\n     -1 \\cdot 7 = -7\n     \\]\n   - Finally, add 12 to \\(-7\\):\n     \\[\n     -7 + 12 = 5\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
